I’ve been looking at how we may better utilise Composer as part of our dependency management, both for Moodle dependencies, and also for things like moodle plugins in the future.

People are already doing the Moodle plugins side of things unofficially so I’m keen to not break their existing workflows, and indeed improve them where possible.

One area which has come up in my discovery phase is the use of a virtual package for {{moodle/moodle}} such that anything depending upon Moodle can also support Moodle-like alternates such as Moodle Workplace.

Essentially this requires us to do:



{code:json}{
    "provide": {
        "moodle/lms": "[version]"
    }
}{code}

We will also have to update this version on every major release.

That is:

* {{dev}} branch should be the _next_ Moodle release - for example we are currently working on Moodle 5.2, so the {{[version]}} should be {{”5.2”}}
* stable branches should use the _current_ Moodle major - for example {{”5.1"}}.



This change is safe to backport to stables and, arguably, should be. If we do no backport then those people who are already trying to do these things with Moodle (they do exist) will struggle to transition to the new approach.

We currently call the following:


{code:php}$resolver = \core\di::get(\Invoker\CallableResolver::class);
$callable = $resolver->resolve($callable);{code}

The resolver does not just resolve a string, but resolves a callable “into a real PHP callable”. What this actually means is that, under the hook, it calls:

{noformat}        if (is_array($callable) && is_string($callable[0])) {
            try {
                // Replace the container entry name by the actual object
                $callable[0] = $this->container->get($callable[0]);
                return $callable;{noformat}

This causes it to be instantiated.

In our case we know that the callable passed is, in 99% of cases, the actual class name, for example:

{noformat}\core\router\util::get_path_for_callable([\core_user\route\api\preferences::class, 'get_preferences'], ['user' => 1);{noformat}

We can easily short-circuit this by doing something like:


{noformat}if (is_string($callable) && strpos($callable, '::') !== false) {
    $callable = explode('::', $callable, 2);
}

if (is_array($callable) && count($callable) === 2) {
    if (class_exists($callable[0]) && method_exists($callable[0], $callable[1])) {
        return $callable[0] . '::' . $callable[1];
    }
}{noformat}



This will still defer to the current approach if the class does not exist, or the method on the class does not exist.

