The {{loginwith}} string introduced in [https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-87546|https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-87546|smart-link] is currently defined as {{'Log in with'}}, which is designed to be concatenated with a provider name in the UI (e.g. "Log in with Google"). This violates [Moodle's coding style policy|https://moodledev.io/general/development/policies/codingstyle#strings], which states that language strings must be able to stand alone and should not be designed for UI concatenation, as this causes difficulties when translating into other languages.

The string should be updated to accept the provider name as a placeholder instead, e.g.: {{$string['loginwith'] = 'Log in with {$a}';}}

This way the full sentence is self-contained and translatable as a unit.

h2. Overview

Following the work in AE-84, we need to provide two AMD modules to give developers a clean, consistent API for working with ESM modules and React components from AMD code.

----

h2. 1. {{core/import}}

A thin AMD module that exposes a dynamic ESM import helper, working around the static analysis restrictions that prevent {{import()}} from being used directly inside AMD modules.

{code:js}export default new Function('specifier', 'return import(specifier)');{code}

This allows AMD callers to do:

{code:js}import nativeImport from 'core/import';
nativeImport('@moodle/lms/mod_book/viewer').then(...);{code}

----

h2. 2. {{core/component}}

An AMD module (or addition to {{core/templates}}) that provides a consistent API for mounting ESM/React components into the DOM by creating the expected container markup.

h3. {{appendToDom(moduleName, properties, appendTo)}}

Creates a {{<div>}} with the appropriate {{data-react-component}} and {{data-react-props}} attributes and appends it to the given element.

{code:js}export const appendToDom = (moduleName, properties, appendTo) => {
    const container = document.createElement('div');
    container.dataset.reactComponent = moduleName;
    container.dataset.reactProps = JSON.stringify(properties);
    appendTo.append(container);
};{code}

h3. {{prependToDom(moduleName, properties, prependTo)}}

Same as {{appendToDom}} but prepends the container instead.

----

h2. Acceptance criteria

* {{core/import}} exists as an AMD module and successfully returns a Promise resolving to an ESM module.
* {{core/component}} (or equivalent location in {{core/templates}}) exposes both {{appendToDom}} and {{prependToDom}}.
* Both functions create the correct container markup recognised by the MutationObserver auto-initialiser.
* Unit/Behat coverage as appropriate.

Modify the import map and page requirements manager to add import map entries for each theme, mapping a pattern like {{@moodle/lms/[themename]}} to a versioned URL such as:

{noformat}https://example.com/sm/core/esm/1776735946/[themename]/@moodle/lms/{noformat}

The current page should also resolve to the active theme's URL, so that:

{noformat}@moodle/lms/core/example => https://example.com/sm/core/esm/1776735946/boost/@moodle/lms/core/example{noformat}

Theme resolution must be deferred to request time — not performed up-front on every page load, as scanning all possible theme override files is an expensive operation.

This also needs to consider whether swizzling of other component libraries (e.g. MDS) should be supported in the same way.

In the page requirements manager, {{react_mustache_autoinit}} currently specifies a file path for the autoinit script. It should instead use the bare module specifier from the import map.

The correct implementation is:

{code:php}public function react_mustache_autoinit(): string {
    return html_writer::tag(
        tagname: 'script',
        contents: 'import "@moodle/lms/core/react_autoinit";',
        attributes: [
            'type' => 'module',
        ],
    );
}{code}

Using a bare specifier (pulled from the import map) is consistent with how other modules are loaded and ensures the correct versioned URL is resolved by the browser at runtime rather than hardcoded in PHP.

User validation takes place in {{public/webservice/lib.php}} in the {{webservice_server::authenticate_user()}} method.

I propose moving the various parts of this out into a new class: {{\core_auth\validate_user}}, breaking up the differenet constituent parts into different methods, for example:

* {{validate_user_maintenance_mode_access($user)}}
* {{validate_user_not_deleted($user)}}
* {{validate_user_is_confirmed($user)}}
* {{validate_user_is_not_suspended($user)}}

We can then have some other methods like:

* {{validate_user_before_external_login($user)}}

Each of these validations should return void or throw a specific exception:

* {{\core_auth\exception\maintenance_mode_enabled_exception($user)}}
* {{\core_auth\exception\user_deleted_exception($user)}}
* {{\core_auth\exception\user_not_confirmed_exception($user)}}
* {{\core_auth\exception\user_suspended_exception($user)}}

I’ve previously created a PoC of this change here: [https://github.com/andrewnicols/moodle/commit/2ddb56f3f2a52bbd066ed52bd7016ba74d294bc8|https://github.com/andrewnicols/moodle/commit/2ddb56f3f2a52bbd066ed52bd7016ba74d294bc8|smart-link], but this may not be complete or correct - especially around the exception handling.

This change relies on [https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88579|https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88579|smart-link] to pass the previous exception in the chain.

I’ve just encountered and issue whereby a change in a parent class causes build changes in the child classes, for example:

Parent class:

{{Base.ts}}

{noformat}export default class Base {
    get(key: string): any {
        return (this as any)[key];
    }
}{noformat}



{{Child.ts}}

{noformat}import Base from './Base';

export default class Child extends Base {
    get(key: string): any {
        return (this as any)[key];
    }
}{noformat}



After building these changes, and staging then, I added a {{set}} method to the {{Base.ts}} too:

{noformat}    set(key: string, value: any): void {
        (this as any)[key] = value;
    }{noformat}



Upon building I now get changes to the Child.min.ts.

This is because esbuild is automatically bundling relative imports. We need to mark all files as {{external}}.

h2. Background

[https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88176|https://moodle.atlassian.net/browse/MDL-88176|smart-link] changed {{core_customfield\handler::create}} from returning a new instance by default to returning a singleton.

In 5.1, the base class explicitly allowed each handler to choose whether to use singleton caching. In 5.2, caching is now imposed on every subclass. Plugins that relied on fresh instances or stored state in their handlers can now potentially leak state between calls.

MDL-88176 was based on the premise that “every extending class” overrode {{create}} to cache a singleton. That is true in core, but not true generally. The [mod_cms|https://github.com/catalyst/moodle-mod_cms):] plugin’s {{cmsuserlist_handler}} [inherits the create method|https://github.com/catalyst/moodle-mod_cms/blob/5d1ba37d0cbd9a88404d725033a7bac4cb7f08c9/classes/customfield/cmsuserlist_handler.php#L32-L36)] and stores the active CMS in the handler, which its [datasource assigns|https://github.com/catalyst/moodle-mod_cms/blob/5d1ba37d0cbd9a88404d725033a7bac4cb7f08c9/classes/local/datasource/userlist.php#L67-L70).] after calling create.

h2. Replication

Add this script to your Moodle root in a 5.1 install and a 5.2 install:

{code:php}<?php

namespace {
    define('CLI_SCRIPT', true);
    require(__DIR__ . '/config.php');
}

namespace local_lifecycletest\customfield {
    use core_customfield\field_controller;
    final class example_handler extends \core_customfield\handler {
        public $value = '';

        public function get_configuration_context(): \context {
            return \context_system::instance();
        }

        public function get_configuration_url(): \moodle_url {
              return new \moodle_url('/');
        }

        public function get_instance_context(int $instanceid = 0): \context {
            return \context_system::instance();
        }

        public function can_configure(): bool {
            return true;
        }

        public function can_edit(field_controller $field, int $instanceid = 0): bool {
            return true;
        }

        public function can_view(field_controller $field, int $instanceid): bool {
            return true;
        }
  }
}

namespace {
    $first = \local_lifecycletest\customfield\example_handler::create();
    $first->value = 'first';

    $second = \local_lifecycletest\customfield\example_handler::create();
    $second->value = 'second';

    echo 'Same instance: ' . ($first === $second ? 'yes' : 'no') . PHP_EOL;
    echo 'First value: ' . $first->value . PHP_EOL;
}{code}

Run it in 5.1:

{noformat}$ php thing.php
Same instance: no
First value: first{noformat}

Run it in 5.2:

{noformat}$ php thing.php
Same instance: yes
First value: second{noformat}

h2. Proposed Fix

This is a backwards compatibility regression in a documented public API. The [Custom fields API docs|https://moodledev.io/docs/5.2/apis/core/customfields] state that “Some areas may choose to return a singleton here”, documenting singleton caching as _optional_. [Moodle's deprecation policy|https://moodledev.io/general/development/policies/deprecation#what-is-moodles-deprecation-policy] treats APIs expected to be called externally or overridden as public, while the [integration review process|https://moodledev.io/general/development/process/integration#integration-review-process] starts from the requirement that backwards compatibility must always be maintained.

So, there is no choice but to restore the pre-5.2 contract. The inherited {{create}} method must return a fresh handler instance, while individual handlers remain free to use singleton caching. The behaviour shipped in 5.2 is a regression and must be corrected rather than becoming the new public API.

We find that {{<String>}} component always do a Suspense suspend/resume, one time, on first render, even when the string we want is already there in {{M.str}} cache.

To produce the bug is to add this test to {{public/lib/js/esm/tests/String.test.tsx}}:

{noformat}it('shows the fallback for an instant before an already-cached string, even with no real fetch', async() => {
        // Cache the string BEFORE rendering, simulating one Moodle already loaded on the page.
        (globalThis as any).mockString('all', 'core', 'All');

        const {container} = render(<String identifier="all" component="core">{'loading'}</String>);

        // Check the DOM right away, before waiting for anything async.
        expect(container.textContent).toBe('loading');
    });{noformat}

The result is passed, but it shouldn't be because the expected result is {{All}}, not {{loading}}

h2. Summary

The blocks drawer skip links (for example, "Skip Table of contents") do not become visible when they receive keyboard focus, even though they are focusable and can be activated. This affects all browsers on main and is a [WCAG 2.4.7 Focus Visible (Level AA)|https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-visible.html] issue.

h2. Problem

Every skip link uses the {{visually-hidden-focusable}} pattern: hidden until focused, then revealed. The site-level "Skip to main content" link reveals correctly on focus. The skip links generated for blocks in the blocks drawer (rendered by {{core/block}}, for example the mod_book "Table of contents" block) do not reveal: the link stays visually hidden even while focused. It can still be activated with Enter and focus moves to the target, so it is a visibility problem only.

h2. Steps to reproduce

# Open an activity that puts a block in the blocks drawer, for example a Book with a couple of chapters (the "Table of contents" block).
# Make sure the blocks drawer is open.
# Using the keyboard, Tab to the "Skip Table of contents" link.

*Expected*: the skip link becomes visible while focused, the same way "Skip to main content" does.

*Actual*: the skip link stays invisible. Nothing appears on screen even though the link is focused (and pressing Enter works).

Reproduces in Chrome, Firefox and Safari on main. Does not reproduce on stable branches.

h2. Affected context

* Branch: main only. It is a regression from MDL-88411; stable branches do not have the anchored-drawer {{clip-path}} and are not affected.
* Browsers: all.
* Layouts: pages where the blocks drawer is anchored, that is {{pagelayout-standard}} and {{body.limitedwidth}}, at the {{lg}} breakpoint and up. mod_book view pages use {{limitedwidth}}, so they are affected.

h2. Root cause

Two CSS rules interact badly:

# The reveal rule pins any focused skip link to the top-left corner of the viewport:
{noformat}// theme/boost/scss/moodle/core.scss
.visually-hidden-focusable:focus,
.visually-hidden-focusable:active {
    position: fixed;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    z-index: 1031;
}{noformat}
# MDL-88411 gave the anchored blocks drawer a {{clip-path}}, used to animate opening and to keep its content within its box:
{noformat}// theme/boost/scss/moodle/drawer.scss (inside .pagelayout-standard, body.limitedwidth)
.drawer.drawer-right.show {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
}{noformat}

{{clip-path}} clips the painting of the whole subtree, including descendants that have escaped layout containment via {{position: fixed}}. So when a drawer skip link is focused and jumps to {{position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0}} (the viewport corner, outside the drawer's box), the drawer's {{clip-path}} clips it away and it is never painted. This is not browser-specific: it was confirmed by forcing the link to {{position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0}} with a bright background, after which the link is present in the DOM with real dimensions but nothing paints at the corner.

"Skip to main content" is never affected because it lives at the top of {{<body>}}, not inside the clipped drawer.

Related existing code: {{drawer.scss}} already carries a workaround for a different symptom of the same {{position: fixed}} reveal (raising the drawer {{z-index}} on {{:focus-within}} so the fixed link is not hidden behind the navbar). That workaround does not address the clip-path clipping.

As part of migrating the Timeline block to React, the PHP code currently mounts the React component by manually generating the required HTML and serialising the component props.

For example:

{code:javascript}$props = json_encode($props, JSON_UNESCAPED_UNICODE | JSON_HEX_TAG);

$mount = html_writer::div('', '', [
    'data-react-component' => '@moodle/lms/block_timeline/Timeline',
    'data-react-props' => $props,
]);{code}

This exposes the implementation details of the React mounting mechanism to feature code. Consumers are required to understand:

*  the required {{data-*}} attributes; 
*  how props should be serialised; 
*  which JSON encoding options should be used; 
*  how the mount element should be constructed. 

This should instead be provided through a supported PHP API so that feature code only needs to specify the React component and its props. The implementation details of the mounting mechanism should remain internal to the frontend platform.

Providing a standard API would:

*  reduce boilerplate when creating React entry points; 
*  ensure consistent prop serialisation and escaping; 
*  hide the DOM contract used by the React autoinit system; 
*  allow the mounting implementation to evolve without requiring changes throughout core and plugins. 

The Timeline block can continue using the current approach until such an API is available, but future React components should be encouraged to use the new abstraction.

A new interface should also be considered to perform in a similar way as {{named_templatable}} to provide a simplified migration path from templates to React. This should allow the react component entrypoint to be generated based upon a renderable being rendered, for example:

{code:php}$renderable = new \block_timeline\output\main($sort, $filter, $limit);
$renderer = $this->page->get_renderer('block_timeline');
$this->content = (object) [
    'text' => $renderer->render($renderable),
    'footer' => '',
];{code}

The react interface should take precedence over the {{named_templatable}} interface, and have lower precedent than a specific {{render_[renderable]}} method.

*Acceptance criteria*

* A supported PHP API exists for mounting React components. 
* Feature code specifies only the component identifier and props. 
* Prop serialisation and escaping are handled internally.
* The DOM contract used by the React autoinit system is hidden from consumers. 
* Existing consumers can be migrated to the new API.
* A renderable react component interface exists.
** Which automatically handles rendering using the PHP API when a renderable is rendered using the {{render}} method
** Which has a higher precedent than {{named_templatable}}
** Which has a lower precedent than a correclty named {{render_[renderable_name]}} method in a renderer class.

The goal of this issue is not to change how React components are mounted, but to provide a stable abstraction over the current implementation so that feature and plugin developers are not coupled to its internal details.

